ecting pure-EKRness for triangulations\n\nWe next have a closer look at the limits of generalizations towards triangulations. The computations discussed before Conjecture 1.5 suggest that the pure-EKR property in Conjecture 1.1, Conjecture 1.5, Conjecture 1.6 and might be strict. Moreover, the complexes are also 2-intersecting pure-EKR for all . However, the following closely related examples show that they are neither strict 2-intersecting pure-EKR nor 3-intersecting pure-EKR in general.\n\nCounterexample 1.21 strict 2-intersecting pure-EKRness for triangulations\n\nConsider triangulations of a heptagon, all of which have four internal diagonals. Any family consisting of one triangulation T and the four triangulations obtained by flipping an inner diagonal in T is a 2-intersecting family of size five which is not a star, e.g.,\n\nImage\n\n  1. Download: Download high-res image (94KB)\n  2. Download: Download full-size image\n\nIn contrast, the star of a face of size two cannot have size larger than five: once we introduce two diagonals into the heptagon we are either left with two quadrilaterals (which yields four possibilities to complete to a triangulation) or with a pentagon (which yields five).\n\nCounterexample 1.22 3-intersecting pure-EKRness for triangulations\n\nConsider triangulations of an octagon, all of which have five internal diagonals. The five triangulations in Counterexample 1.21 joined with the edge 1—7 form a 3-intersecting family. Since the starting triangulation shares 4 edges with all others, one can as well flip the additional edge 1—7 to obtain another triangulation, producing a 3-intersecting family of size six in total, e.g.,\n\nImage\n\n  1. Download: Download high-res image (97KB)\n  2. Download: Download full-size image\n\nIn contrast, the star of a face of size three cannot have size larger than five.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nErdős–Ko–Rado theorem (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erd%C5%91s%E2%80%93Ko%E2%80%93Rado_theorem)\nciteturn8search15 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; An unproven conjecture, posed by Gil Kalai and Karen Meagher, concerns another analog for the family of triangulations of a convex polygon with ${\\displaystyle n}$ vertices. ... * Friedgut, Ehud (2008), \"On the measure of intersecting families, uniqueness and stability\" (PDF), Combinatorica, 28 (5): 503–528, doi:10.1007/s00493-008-2318-9, MR 2501247, S2CID 7225916, Zbl 1199.05319, archived from the original (PDF) on 2021-01-25 ... * Olarte, Jorge Alberto; Santos, Francisco; Spreer, Jonathan; Stump, Christian (2020), \"The EKR property for flag pure simplicial complexes without boundary\" (PDF), Journal of Combinatorial Theory, Series A, 172 105205: 105205, 29, arXiv:1710.02518, doi:10.1016/j.jcta.2019.105205, MR 4052307, S2CID 119158469\nThe largest intersecting families are obtained by choosing one position and a fixed symbol for that position, and letting the rest of the positions vary arbitrarily. These families consist of ${\\textstyle q^{n-1}}$ strings, and are the only pairwise intersecting families of this size. More generally, the largest families of strings in which every two have ${\\displaystyle t}$ positions with equal symbols are obtained by choosing ${\\displaystyle t+2i}$ positions and symbols for those positions, for a number ${\\displaystyle i}$ that depends on ${\\displaystyle n}$, ${\\displaystyle q}$, and ${\\displaystyle t}$, and constructing the family of strings that each have at least ${\\displaystyle t+i}$ of the chosen symbols. These results can be interpreted graph-theoretically in terms of the Hamming scheme.\n\nUnsolved problem in mathematics\n\nIs the largest family of intersecting triangulations of a convex polygon obtained by cutting off one vertex and choosing all triangulations of the remaining polygon?\n\nMore unsolved problems in mathematics\n\nAn unproven conjecture, posed by Gil Kalai and Karen Meagher, concerns another analog for the family of triangulations of a convex polygon with ${\\displaystyle n}$ vertices. The number of all triangulations is a Catalan number ${\\displaystyle C_{n-2}}$, and the conjecture states that a family of triangulations every pair of which shares an edge has maximum size ${\\displaystyle C_{n-3}}$. An intersecting family of size exactly ${\\displaystyle C_{n-3}}$ may be obtained by cutting off a single vertex of the polygon by a triangle, and choosing all ways of triangulating the remaining ${\\displaystyle (n-1)}$-vertex polygon.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCentres médico-psychopédagogique (CMPP) (https://www.psycom.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/PSYCOM_GS78_web.pdf)\nciteturn8search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.5 years ago; Centres médico-psychopédagogique (CMPP) ... 23, boulevard Gambetta\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Problem Atlas — Coragora (https://coragora.com/atlas)\nciteturn8search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; We collect problem statements, sources and progress records from the Erdős problems catalog, Math Stack Exchange, Smale’s problem list, and results reported by AI labs and the people working with them (OpenAI’s and Google DeepMind’s 2026 releases, among others). ... Erdős #90 (unit distances) shown closed: disproved with an AI-found construction, checked by people (arXiv:2605.20695).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJonathan Rollin - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/author/jonathan-rollin)\nciteturn8search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Simona Boyadzhiyska, Dennis Clemens, Pranshu Gupta, Jonathan Rollin ... In addition, we establish that for $(T,H)$ to be Ramsey equivalent to $(T,K_t)$, $H$ must have roughly this form. ... For connected crossing ordered graphs H we reduce the problem of determining whether f(H) is finite to a family o